/* data/learn-c/phy-12-15.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-12-15.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-12-15"] = {"15.1":{"s":"The idea that matter is made of atoms is very old, from the Greek thinkers to Dalton. This chapter follows how experiments slowly showed what is inside an atom. First came electrons, then a tiny nucleus and fixed energy levels. Last came the nucleus itself and radioactivity.","w":["Each model of the atom was built to fix a flaw in the one before it. Know which flaw each model solved.","The nucleus is tiny compared with the atom, but it holds almost all the mass."],"x":["The idea that matter is made of small particles is very old. Dalton gave it a scientific form, as atoms that cannot be divided. Then experiments showed that the atom has parts, and that its inside has a structure.","This chapter follows the story in order. The electron was found first. Then the scattering of alpha particles showed a tiny nucleus. Then the line spectrum of hydrogen showed that the electrons have only certain energies, and Bohr built a model for it. At the end of the chapter, we look inside the nucleus, and study radioactivity, binding energy, fission and fusion.","Study tip: learn each model with the experiment that supports it and the fact that it could not explain. This is a common question in the board paper."],"e":"Order of ideas: Dalton's solid atom, then Thomson's plum-pudding atom, then Rutherford's nuclear atom, then Bohr's atom with fixed orbits. Each one fixed a fault of the one before."},"15.2":{"s":"After discovering the electron, Thomson pictured the atom as a sphere of spread-out positive charge with electrons stuck in it, like seeds in a watermelon. The model kept the atom neutral, but it could not explain the scattering experiment that followed.","w":["Thomson's model could not explain why a few alpha particles bounce straight back.","In his model the positive charge is spread out; there is no nucleus."],"x":["After the electron was found, it was clear that an atom, which is neutral, must also hold a positive charge. Thomson proposed a model in which the positive charge is spread evenly over a sphere of the size of the atom, and the small electrons are embedded in it, like seeds in a watermelon.","In this model the atom is neutral as a whole, and the electrons can vibrate about their places. The model is simple and it explained why atoms are neutral.","But it could not explain what happened when alpha particles were fired at a thin foil. A positive charge that is spread out is a weak field, and it could not turn a fast alpha particle through a large angle. So the model was dropped.","Common mistake: thinking that Thomson found the nucleus. He did not. The nucleus was found by Rutherford and his team."],"e":"Thomson's atom with the charge spread over about 10⁻¹⁰ m would deflect an alpha particle only by a tiny angle. But a few alpha particles were turned through more than 90°, and the model could not give this."},"15.3":{"s":"Geiger and Marsden fired alpha particles at a very thin gold foil. Almost all went straight through. A few were deflected through large angles, and a very small number bounced almost straight back. Thomson's spread-out charge could never turn an alpha particle back.","w":["Most alpha particles going straight through shows the atom is mostly empty space."],"x":["In the experiment of Geiger and Marsden, done under Rutherford, a narrow beam of alpha particles from a radioactive source fell on a very thin gold foil. A detector, a screen that gave a flash for each particle, was moved round the foil to count the particles at different angles.","The results: most of the alpha particles went straight through, with very little deviation. A small number were deflected through large angles. A very small number, about one in 8000 for a thin foil, were turned back nearly in the opposite direction.","The meaning: because most particles are not deflected, most of the atom is empty space. Because a few are turned back, there must be a very strong electric field in a tiny region, which holds the positive charge and most of the mass. Thomson's model could not give this.","Common mistake: thinking that the alpha particles bounced off the electrons. An alpha particle is about 7000 times as heavy as an electron, and it is hardly affected by electrons."],"e":"If every atom were a solid ball, as Thomson thought, all alpha particles would be deflected a little. In the experiment, most were not deflected at all. So the atom has a large empty space, and the charge is packed into a very small region."},"15.4":{"s":"Rutherford concluded that all the positive charge and almost all the mass sit in a tiny central nucleus, with electrons orbiting far outside. The atom is mostly empty space. But an orbiting electron should radiate energy and spiral into the nucleus, so the model could not explain why atoms are stable.","f":[{"x":"r₀ = 2kZe²/K","n":"Rutherford distance of closest approach. Alpha particle of kinetic energy K, head-on."}],"w":["Rutherford's model fails on two counts: atoms are stable, and they give line (not continuous) spectra."],"x":["Rutherford studied the pattern of scattering and gave a nuclear model. All the positive charge and almost all the mass of the atom are in a tiny nucleus, of size about 10⁻¹⁵ m. The electrons move round the nucleus in orbits, like planets round the Sun, held by the electric attraction. The atom has a size of about 10⁻¹⁰ m, so it is mostly empty.","The closest an alpha particle can get to the nucleus is found by equating its kinetic energy with the electric potential energy. This gave an upper limit to the size of the nucleus.","The model has two serious faults. First, an electron moving in a circle is accelerating, and by classical physics it should radiate energy and spiral into the nucleus in a very short time. But atoms are stable. Second, the model predicts a continuous spectrum, but atoms give line spectra with fixed wavelengths.","These faults were removed by Bohr's model, which added new rules for the electron."],"e":"Compare sizes: the nucleus is about 10⁻¹⁵ m and the atom is about 10⁻¹⁰ m. The ratio is 10⁵. If the atom were as big as a stadium, the nucleus would be about the size of a small seed."},"15.5":{"s":"Hot hydrogen gas does not give a continuous rainbow. It gives sharp lines at fixed wavelengths: a line spectrum. The lines fall into series (Lyman, Balmer, Paschen and others), and their wavelengths follow a simple formula with the Rydberg constant.","f":[{"x":"1/lambda = R*(1/n^2 - 1/m^2)","n":"Lines of hydrogen; m > n. n = 1 Lyman (UV), 2 Balmer (visible), 3 Paschen (IR), then Brackett, Pfund.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.5, p.326"}],"w":["The series limit is the shortest wavelength of a series (m → ∞): λ = n²/R."],"x":["A hot solid gives a continuous spectrum, with all the colours. But a gas that is excited, for example by an electric discharge, gives only a few bright lines at fixed wavelengths. This is a line spectrum, and each element has its own, so it is like a fingerprint.","The lines of hydrogen fall into groups called series. Each series ends at a fixed lower level n: Lyman (n = 1, ultraviolet), Balmer (n = 2, visible), Paschen (n = 3, infrared), Brackett (n = 4) and Pfund (n = 5). The wavelengths follow the formula 1/λ = R(1/n² − 1/m²), where m is a whole number larger than n, and R = 1.097 × 10⁷ m⁻¹ is the Rydberg constant.","For the longest wavelength of a series, take m = n + 1. For the shortest wavelength, called the series limit, take m = ∞, so 1/λ = R/n².","Common mistake: using the wrong lower level for a series, or using m smaller than n."],"e":"Series limit of the Balmer series: n = 2, m = ∞, so 1/λ = R/4. λ = 4/(1.097 × 10⁷) = 3.65 × 10⁻⁷ m = 365 nm, in the ultraviolet, at the edge of the visible region."},"15.6":{"s":"Bohr's postulates: electrons move only in certain orbits, without radiating. In these orbits angular momentum is nh/2π. Light is emitted or absorbed only when an electron jumps between orbits, with hν equal to the energy difference. For hydrogen, orbit radius grows as n² and energy is −13.6/n² eV. This explains the spectral series.","f":[{"x":"m_e*v_n*r_n = n*h/(2*pi)","n":"Bohr's second postulate: angular momentum is quantised.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.6, p.327"},{"x":"r_n = a_0*n^2","n":"Orbit radius in hydrogen; Bohr radius a_0 = 0.053 nm.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.6, p.327"},{"x":"E_n = -13.6*Z^2/n^2 eV","n":"Energy of the electron in the n-th orbit (Z = 1 for hydrogen).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.6, p.328"},{"x":"1/lambda = R_H*Z^2*(1/n^2 - 1/m^2)","n":"Rydberg's formula from Bohr's model; R_H = 1.097 × 10^7 per metre.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.6, p.329"},{"x":"2*pi*r_n = n*lambda_n","n":"De Broglie: a whole number of electron waves fits round each allowed orbit.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.6, p.330"}],"w":["Energy is negative: the electron is bound. Ionisation energy of hydrogen = +13.6 eV.","First excitation energy of hydrogen: −3.4 − (−13.6) = 10.2 eV."],"x":["Bohr's model for the hydrogen atom has three postulates. (1) An electron moves in certain circular orbits without radiating. (2) Only those orbits are allowed in which the angular momentum is a whole-number multiple of h/2π: mvr = nh/2π. (3) Energy is given out or taken in only when the electron jumps between two orbits, and the photon has hν = E_i − E_f.","The results: the radius of the n-th orbit is r_n = a₀n²/Z, with the Bohr radius a₀ = 0.053 nm. The energy is E_n = −13.6 Z²/n² eV. The energy is negative because the electron is bound. The ground state is n = 1, with −13.6 eV for hydrogen, and the ionisation energy is +13.6 eV.","A jump from a higher level to a lower level emits a photon, and it gives the spectral lines. The first excitation energy of hydrogen, from n = 1 to n = 2, is 13.6 − 3.4 = 10.2 eV. The model also agrees with de Broglie's idea: a whole number of electron waves fit round each allowed orbit, 2πr = nλ.","The model works only for hydrogen-like ions with one electron (H, He⁺, Li²⁺). Common mistake: forgetting the Z² in the energy of He⁺ and Li²⁺."],"e":"Energy needed to take the electron of He⁺ (Z = 2) from n = 1 to infinity: E₁ = −13.6 × 4 = −54.4 eV, so the ionisation energy is 54.4 eV. Radius of the first orbit: 0.053/2 = 0.0265 nm."},"15.7":{"s":"The nucleus is made of protons and neutrons, together called nucleons. Z is the number of protons (atomic number) and A is the number of nucleons (mass number). Isotopes have the same Z but different A. Nuclear radius grows as A^(1/3), so all nuclei have about the same density. A strong, short-range nuclear force holds nucleons together.","f":[{"x":"A = Z + N","n":"Mass number = protons + neutrons.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.7, p.331"},{"x":"1 u = 931.5 MeV/c^2","n":"Atomic mass unit (1/12 of a carbon-12 atom) as energy.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.7, p.331"},{"x":"R = R_0*A^(1/3)","n":"Nuclear radius; R_0 = 1.2 × 10^-15 m.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.7, p.332"}],"w":["Isotopes: same Z. Isobars: same A. Isotones: same N."],"x":["The nucleus has protons and neutrons, which together are called nucleons. The number of protons is the atomic number Z. The number of nucleons is the mass number A, so the number of neutrons is N = A − Z. A nucleus of an element X is written ᴬ_Z X.","Isotopes have the same Z and different A, like ³⁵Cl and ³⁷Cl. Isobars have the same A and different Z. Isotones have the same N.","Masses of atoms are given in atomic mass units, u. One u is one twelfth of the mass of a carbon-12 atom, and 1 u is equivalent to 931.5 MeV of energy.","The radius of a nucleus is R = R₀A^(1/3), with R₀ = 1.2 × 10⁻¹⁵ m. The volume is proportional to A, and so is the mass. This means that all nuclei have nearly the same density, about 10¹⁷ kg/m³. The nucleons are held by the strong nuclear force. It is attractive, has a short range, and is almost the same between any two nucleons.","Common mistake: mixing the three words isotopes, isobars and isotones. Compare Z, A and N."],"e":"Radius of an iron nucleus, A = 56: R = 1.2 × 56^(1/3) = 1.2 × 3.83 = 4.6 × 10⁻¹⁵ m. The radius of the uranium nucleus, A = 238, is 1.2 × 6.2 = 7.4 × 10⁻¹⁵ m."},"15.8":{"s":"A nucleus has less mass than its separate protons and neutrons. The missing mass (mass defect) has become binding energy: E = Δm c², with 1 u equal to about 931.5 MeV. Binding energy per nucleon is highest near iron, so splitting very heavy nuclei or joining very light ones releases energy.","f":[{"x":"Delta M = Z*m_p + N*m_n - M","n":"Mass defect of a nucleus of mass M.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.8, p.333"},{"x":"E_B = (Z*m_H + N*m_n - M)*c^2","n":"Binding energy using atomic masses: m_H = mass of a hydrogen atom, M = mass of the whole atom (not the bare nucleus).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.8, p.333"}],"w":["Compare stability by binding energy PER NUCLEON, not total binding energy."],"x":["The mass of a stable nucleus is always less than the sum of the masses of its separate protons and neutrons. The difference is the mass defect, Δm = Zm_p + Nm_n − M. When the nucleons come together, this mass is given out as energy, the binding energy: E_B = Δm c². With the mass in u, E_B = Δm × 931.5 MeV.","The binding energy is also the work needed to pull the nucleus apart into free nucleons. When atomic masses are used, count the hydrogen atom mass in place of the proton mass, so the electrons cancel.","To compare how stable nuclei are, use the binding energy per nucleon, E_B/A. It rises quickly for light nuclei, has a broad peak of about 8.7 MeV near iron (A about 56), and falls slowly for heavy nuclei. So energy is released when a very heavy nucleus splits (fission), or when very light nuclei join (fusion).","Common mistake: comparing the total binding energy. A big nucleus has a large total, but this does not make it more stable."],"e":"Helium-4: Δm = 0.030377 u, so E_B = 0.030377 × 931.5 = 28.3 MeV. Per nucleon, it is 28.3/4 = 7.07 MeV."},"15.9":{"s":"Unstable nuclei decay. Alpha decay emits a helium nucleus (A falls by 4, Z by 2). Beta-minus decay turns a neutron into a proton and emits an electron and an antineutrino (Z rises by 1). Gamma decay emits a high-energy photon and changes neither A nor Z.","f":[{"x":"Q = (m_X - m_Y - m_He)*c^2","n":"Energy released in alpha decay (atomic masses).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.9, p.334"}],"w":["Beta-minus: a neutron becomes a proton, so A stays the same and Z rises by 1."],"x":["An unstable nucleus changes into a more stable one by radioactive decay. There are three kinds. In alpha decay, the nucleus emits an alpha particle, a helium nucleus. Z falls by 2 and A falls by 4. For example, ²³⁸U gives ²³⁴Th and ⁴He.","In beta-minus decay, a neutron in the nucleus changes into a proton, and an electron and an antineutrino are emitted. Z increases by 1, and A does not change. In gamma decay, an excited nucleus goes to a lower energy level, and gives out a photon. Neither Z nor A changes.","To write a decay equation, balance the sums of the A values and the sums of the Z values on both sides. The energy released, Q, is found from the mass difference: for alpha decay Q = (m_X − m_Y − m_He)c², with the atomic masses. If Q is positive, the decay can happen. The energy is shared among the products as kinetic energy.","Common mistake: saying that the electron in beta decay was in the nucleus before. It is created when the neutron changes into a proton."],"e":"Carbon-14 beta decay: ¹⁴₆C gives ¹⁴₇N + e⁻ + antineutrino. A stays 14, Z goes from 6 to 7. The sums are 14 = 14 + 0 and 6 = 7 − 1."},"15.10":{"s":"Decay is random, but the rate is proportional to the number of nuclei present: N = N₀ e^(−λt). The half-life, T½ = 0.693/λ, is the time for half the nuclei to decay. Mean life is 1/λ. Activity, the decays per second, is measured in becquerel; 1 curie is 3.7 × 10¹⁰ Bq.","f":[{"x":"N = N_0*e^(-lambda*t)","n":"Law of radioactive decay; lambda = decay constant.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.10, p.336"},{"x":"A = lambda*N = A_0*e^(-lambda*t)","n":"Activity (decays per second, becquerel).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.10, p.336"},{"x":"T_(1/2) = 0.693/lambda","n":"Half-life.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.10, p.337"},{"x":"tau = 1/lambda","n":"Average (mean) life; T_(1/2) = 0.693 tau.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 15.10, p.337"}],"w":["After n half-lives the fraction left is (1/2)ⁿ; it never reaches exactly zero."],"x":["The decay of a single nucleus cannot be predicted, because it is random. But for a large number of nuclei, the rate of decay is proportional to the number N that are left: dN/dt = −λN. The solution is N = N₀e^(−λt), where λ is the decay constant. This is the law of radioactive decay.","The half-life T½ is the time in which half of the nuclei decay: T½ = 0.693/λ. After n half-lives, the fraction left is (1/2)ⁿ. The mean life is τ = 1/λ = T½/0.693, which is longer than T½, about 1.44 T½. It is the time in which N falls to 1/e.","The activity is the number of decays per second: A = λN = A₀e^(−λt). Its SI unit is the becquerel (1 Bq = 1 decay per second). The older unit is the curie, 1 Ci = 3.7 × 10¹⁰ Bq. The activity also halves in each half-life.","Common mistake: thinking that all the nuclei are gone after two half-lives. After two half-lives, a quarter is still left."],"e":"A sample of half-life 8 days is observed for 24 days. This is 3 half-lives, and the fraction left is 1/8. If the starting activity is 800 Bq, it is 100 Bq after 24 days."},"15.11":{"s":"Nuclear energy comes from mass turning into energy. In fission, a heavy nucleus such as U-235 absorbs a neutron and splits, releasing energy and more neutrons. That allows a chain reaction, which is controlled in a reactor. In fusion, light nuclei join at extremely high temperature. Fusion powers the Sun.","f":[{"x":"Q = (mass of reactants − mass of products) × 931.5 MeV","n":"Q value of a nuclear reaction. Positive Q: energy released."},{"x":"B.E./A","n":"Binding energy per nucleon. Maximum, about 8.7 MeV, near iron-56."},{"x":"Fission ≈ 200 MeV per U-235 nucleus ; D–T fusion ≈ 17.6 MeV","n":"Energy from fission and fusion"}],"w":["Fission splits a heavy nucleus; fusion joins light ones. Do not swap them.","In both, the energy comes from a small loss of mass: E = mc squared."],"x":["Nuclear energy comes from the conversion of a little mass to energy by E = Δm c². In nuclear fission, a heavy nucleus, like U-235, absorbs a slow neutron and splits into two lighter nuclei, with two or three more neutrons. About 200 MeV is released in each fission.","The new neutrons can cause more fissions, so a chain reaction can build up. In a bomb, it is not controlled. In a reactor, it is controlled. A moderator, like heavy water or graphite, slows the neutrons down. Control rods, made of a material like cadmium or boron, absorb neutrons to keep the rate steady. A coolant carries the heat away to make steam.","In nuclear fusion, two very light nuclei join to form a heavier one, as in the Sun, where hydrogen changes to helium. It needs a temperature of millions of kelvin, so that the nuclei can overcome their electric repulsion and come within the range of the nuclear force.","Common mistake: saying that a moderator absorbs neutrons. It slows them, and it is the control rod that absorbs them."],"e":"Fission of 1 kg of U-235 gives about 8 × 10¹³ J, which is about 2.7 million times as much as the heat from burning 1 kg of coal, about 3 × 10⁷ J."}};
